# The variable list, as a file you can copy:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored; this file is committed, so nothing in it may be a
# real secret. The one value it does carry is the local container's, which is
# published to loopback and guards nothing. Next.js reads `.env.local`
# automatically; `drizzle.config.ts` and `src/db/migrate.mts` read it
# explicitly, because they are separate processes that load nothing on their own.
#
# The schema this mirrors is `src/lib/env-schema.mts`, and it is checked when the
# server starts rather than when something first reads a value — a missing or
# malformed variable is a startup error naming the variable, not a TypeError in a
# request handler an hour into a deploy. A variable added to one of these two
# files and not the other is half a change.
#
# README.md, under "Environment variables", says where each value comes from in
# each environment.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only on the server, and never sent to a browser. `src/server/env.ts` is
# `server-only`, so a Client Component that imports it fails the build.
#
# Anything secret belongs in this section and nowhere else.

# Postgres. Local Postgres comes from docker-compose.yml — `npm run db:up`
# starts it, and the value below is the one it is configured with, so this line
# works unedited.
#
# These are the committed development credentials, deliberately. The database
# holds no real data and is published to loopback only. Never reuse the password
# anywhere reachable from off the machine.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://capexwise:capexwise_local_dev@127.0.0.1:5432/capexwise


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only by the integration suite, never by the server. It is deliberately a
# separate variable from DATABASE_URL rather than a reuse of it, because the two
# mean opposite things: one names a database to keep, the other names one the
# suite empties before every single test.
#
# Leave it unset. Unset, the suite uses the local compose database with `_test`
# appended and creates it on first run, which is what `npm run db:up` provides.
# Set it only to point the suite at a different server — and note that
# `src/test/db.ts` refuses any value whose database name does not end in `_test`,
# so a copy of the line above will be rejected rather than truncated.
#
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://capexwise:capexwise_local_dev@127.0.0.1:5432/capexwise_test


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are none yet, and the section is here so the first one lands in the
# right half of the file rather than next to the database password.
#
# A variable named `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is not merely conventionally public: the
# compiler inlines its value into the client bundle as a literal, so it is
# published to every browser and stays published in every cached build. A secret
# that acquires the prefix cannot be un-published by removing it. Nothing in the
# Server section above may be renamed into this one to make it reachable from a
# Client Component — pass the value down from a Server Component instead.
#
# `src/lib/env-schema.mts` refuses to start the server if a server variable
# carries the prefix.
